Question 4
An inner join includes only valid employee-department matches. A left join from Employee keeps all employees and shows NULL department values when no row matches. A full join additionally keeps empty departments. For department counts I would start from Department and left join Employee, using COUNT(employee.emp_id), not COUNT(*).

Employee can self-left-join to Manager using e.manager_id=m.emp_id, retaining the CEO with NULL manager. Cross join makes every employee-department pair, so its size is E times D.

Use left join for the all-employee report, department-left-join for the count report, and self join for manager names. A filter on Department in WHERE can remove the NULL rows; it should be in ON when preservation is required. My SQL syntax examples are abbreviated.

